      <memberdef kind="function" id="classcsmp_1_1_t_r_i_a_n_g_l_e___interface_1a026c03678096ec4e8b149d71684a3cac" prot="public" static="no" const="no" explicit="no" inline="no" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>void</type>
        <definition>void csmp::TRIANGLE_Interface::SplitSingleCornerElements</definition>
        <argsstring>(std::map&lt; size_t, std::vector&lt; size_t &gt; &gt; &amp;plist, std::map&lt; size_t, std::vector&lt; int64_t &gt; &gt; &amp;pfverts, std::vector&lt; double &gt; &amp;evalues)</argsstring>
        <name>SplitSingleCornerElements</name>
        <qualifiedname>csmp::TRIANGLE_Interface::SplitSingleCornerElements</qualifiedname>
        <param>
          <type>std::map&lt; size_t, std::vector&lt; size_t &gt; &gt; &amp;</type>
          <declname>plist</declname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>std::map&lt; size_t, std::vector&lt; int64_t &gt; &gt; &amp;</type>
          <declname>pfverts</declname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>std::vector&lt; double &gt; &amp;</type>
          <declname>evalues</declname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>If a 2D triangular finite element has two of its sides on the boundary of the model, this poses problems when boundary conditions are assigned because one always first has to identify the correct model side and secondly because elements rarely are compliant enough to satisfactorily handle the different conditions. <linebreak/>
</para>
<para>The remedy used here, is to find the elements element neighbor and to create a segment from the corner node and the node diametrically opposite. This segment then separates two new elements which are created by flipping the two adjacent elements, example lower left corner: <linebreak/>
</para>
<para>o--<mdash/>o o--<mdash/>o element 2 also becomes a corner element | \ 2 | | 1 / | | \ | --&gt; | / | | 1 \ | | / 2 | o--<mdash/>o o--<mdash/>o</para>
<para>If element properties were assigned before, these are averaged between the two flipped elements and then re-assigned. <linebreak/>
</para>
<sect1 id="group___c_s_m_pglobal_functions_1arguments">
<title>Input Arguments</title><para>The method uses the &apos;plist&apos;, &apos;pfverts&apos;, and potential element property values in the process of flipping the nodes. <linebreak/>
</para>
</sect1>
<sect1 id="namespacecsmp_1implementation">
<title>Implementation</title><para><orderedlist>
<listitem><para>In the pfverts array those elements are singled out which have only one neighbor. These elements must lie at the mesh corners.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>If such an element is found, its neighbor is found as well in the plist. The element ID&apos;s are remembered.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>A segment (MJL_Edge) is constructed from the corner node to the node diametrically opposite in the triangle.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Depending which mesh corner we are in, the two remaining nodes left and right of the new segment form corner nodes of the new triangles (MJL_Triangle).</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>The new triangles are reassigned to the plist and the properties, if present are averaged and mapped back onto the elements. If both property values were the same, nothing is done.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>The elements pfverts are re-assigned according to the new connectivity.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>The elements adjacent to the flipped elements must be updated with regard to their neighbor lists.</para>
</listitem></orderedlist>
</para>
</sect1>
<sect1 id="group___c_s_m_pglobal_enums_1application">
<title>Application Examples</title><para>The method is applied before the <ref refid="classcsmp_1_1_v_set" kindref="compound">VSet</ref> is output from the 2D mesh reading method above. <linebreak/>
</para>
</sect1>
<sect1 id="group___c_s_m_pglobal_functions_1messages">
<title>Messages</title><para>The method reports how many edge flips were performed and whether and which property values were averaged at which mesh corner. <linebreak/>
 <verbatim>    @todo (3) Messes up neighbor connectivity SKM12/5/02
    O.K. for trianglePatch8 retested by SKM 27/11/02 (previously reported error must be in Triangle mesher)
</verbatim> </para>
</sect1>
        </detaileddescription>
